.{
    .name = .qmsg,
    .version = "0.8.3",
    .fingerprint = 0x650c9e5831497a83,
    .minimum_zig_version = "0.17.0",
    .paths = .{
        "build.zig",
        "build.zig.zon",
        "src",
        "examples",
        "docs",
        "README.md",
        "CHANGELOG.md",
        "DESIGN.md",
        "WIRE.md",
        "AUTH.md",
        "ROADMAP.md",
    },
    .dependencies = .{
        .paseto = .{
            .url = "https://github.com/nullstyle/paseto-zig/archive/refs/tags/0.4.0.tar.gz",
            .hash = "paseto-0.4.0-EBI95RcXCADs0jz3tebS1xMaAiT2r3Tpx8HsF5LfpqOS",
        },
        // v0.32.0 (tag at ffdb251): three releases in one move, the set
        // (http3-zig, capnp-zig, qmesh-zig, nest, mruby-quic) with it on
        // 2026-10-07 (v0.31.1 skipped as a step, not as content). No wire
        // change, no API change, the same option map. v0.31.0: a client
        // confirms the handshake on an ACK of its own 1-RTT packet, and
        // `Server.feed` leaves a `.dropped` datagram as it came. v0.31.1:
        // the idle timer follows RFC 9000 section 10.1, so a dead peer's
        // connection ends one idle timeout after the first probe, not
        // three (qmsg found that regression of v0.30.1 with its dead-peer
        // gate). v0.32.0: the receive window an endpoint keeps open is the
        // one it announced (before, a fixed 1 MiB per stream and 16 MiB
        // per connection after the initial window): qmsg's bidi streams
        // and connection announce exactly those, no change there; its
        // unidirectional streams announce 64 KiB (`QuicOptions`), which is
        // now their window for life, not 1 MiB after the first 64 KiB.
        // Also `max_buffered_send` on Connection, Client.Config and
        // Server.Config (the sender's window per stream, default 1 MiB,
        // qmsg sets none), 64 ACK ranges in a frame (was 16), and the
        // loss thresholds that widen for reordering shrink back after 16
        // clean round trips. Before it:
        // v0.30.1 (tag at ccf6ae2). Behavior only: no wire change, no API
        // change, the same option map. A probe timeout is not a loss (RFC
        // 9002 section 6.2.4): the Application space's probe timeout no
        // longer declares the oldest packet lost nor cuts the congestion
        // window; the probe carries its frames again, and the thresholds
        // decide when its ACK comes. The handshake's probe timeout is
        // bounded at about a second and runs from the last send, so a
        // client waiting for a lost server flight probes every second.
        // `quic.unixWallClockUs` compiles on Zig 0.17.0 now, and
        // `Server.adoptLoopThread()` is new; qmsg calls neither. Not
        // v0.30.0: it did not compile on Windows. The set (http3-zig,
        // capnp-zig, qmesh-zig, nest, mruby-quic) moved with it on
        // 2026-10-06. Before it:
        // v0.29.0 (tag at b9a15e6). It holds v0.25.0's security fix (one
        // short datagram from anyone who saw a packet of the connection, or
        // one a small receive buffer cut short, made `Connection.handle`
        // fail and the connection close; such a packet is now dropped) and
        // v0.26.0's repairs: a peer that leaves a connection ID out of its
        // transport parameters is refused, no handshake datagram is longer
        // than 1200 bytes, a close during the handshake reaches the peer,
        // and a key update waits for the confirmed handshake. v0.27.0 adds
        // session tickets that live through a server restart; qmsg sets
        // none of the new fields. v0.28.0 keeps the end of a stream that a
        // `tick` reclaimed: `quic.app` reports it as `.fin` or `.reset`,
        // not `.reaped`, and a stream the app stopped ends as `.reaped`.
        // qmsg reads before `tick`, and it stops a stream only when it
        // cancels the request on it. v0.28.1 is a 32-bit build fix. v0.29.0
        // takes a late packet as late, not lost, costs about 91 KB per
        // connection (was 1.09 MB), and connects through handshake loss; no
        // wire change, and qmsg sets none of its new `Server.Config`
        // fields. The package accepts `optimize` too (since v0.24.1), which
        // the shared map does not pass. Since v0.24.0 there is no lifetime
        // stream cap.
        // The stream limit is a window of streams open at once, and an id
        // comes back only when its stream is closed in both directions, so
        // qmsg opens local streams in id order and waits on
        // StreamLimitExceeded. It ends the send half of every stream it
        // opens (FIN, or a reset when canceled) and the reply half of a
        // no_reply message. An accepted request's stream closes with its
        // reply; a request that gets no reply stays open until the
        // requester's deadline or cancel ends it. Its floor is the tagged Zig
        // 0.17.0. A binary that links qmsg with nest, qmesh-zig, capnp-zig,
        // http3-zig or mruby-quic has one quic module only when every one
        // of them pins this same tag and passes the same option map (see
        // build.zig).
        // v0.33.0 (2026-10-07): cluster A move; the quic release note has what changed.
        .quic = .{
            .url = "https://github.com/nullstyle/quic-zig/archive/refs/tags/v0.33.0.tar.gz",
            .hash = "quic-0.33.0-DnSYvfk6PQAwBH4pHnhv97zhZeAoJ_0JZRjDIPu_jG8u",
        },
        // Optional Cap'n Proto body codec dependency (`-Dcapnp=true`).
        // LAZY: default builds and non-opting consumers never fetch or
        // compile capnp-zig. Same tarball hash the release validation
        // recorded (github.com/nullstyle/capnp-zig/releases/tag/v0.17.0).
        .capnp = .{
            .url = "https://github.com/nullstyle/capnp-zig/archive/refs/tags/v0.17.0.tar.gz",
            .hash = "capnpc_zig-0.17.0-nUduFZYwNwDznNpR3qy-IV_-gehdwsAEFNJtMHTYxVJx",
            .lazy = true,
        },
    },
}
